hi there Steve Kaufman here today I'm  gonna talk I'm gonna stray a little bit  not too far from the subject of language  learning and talk a little bit about  globalization the doubles conference  that's on right now and language  learning and remember if you enjoy these  videos please subscribe so what inspired  this is I'm watching when were down here  in Palm Springs my wife and I tend to  watch cable news along with our coffee  and toast for breakfast hmm and so this  morning I see the pictures of President  Trump in Davos where he's greeted like a  rock star everybody wants to selfie with  him because everybody's been basically  given such a large dosage of Trump in  the news that to see him in you know  live so to speak is is an event but you  know Davos globalization we hear so much  about globalization it's often painted  negatively that it's led to job losses  it's led to I don't know loss of  national identity and stuff which none  of it I believe I happen to believe that  globalization is inevitable historically  there's been this process of increasing  interconnectivity around the world and  what I find attractive what I enjoy  about the world we live in is just the  extent to which we can connect with  people from different parts of the world  and different languages and stuff like  that but Davos is kind of the you know  the the pinnacle the event the society  event of all the wealthy business people  and bankers and political people who  want to go there to be seen and and  again it reminds me of again this whole  idea of interconnectivity the wealthy  people who are their bankers business  people and stuff like that they are  there they are wealthy because of other  you can't be wealthy without having  access to raw materials which in most  cases you didn't put in the ground or  plant you can't be wealthy without  employees  some of whom work for miserable wages  you can't be wealthy without customers  we are all interconnected insofar as  trade products are made you know they  ship papayas from I don't know Mexico  Philippines and product is made in China  and and certain you know minerals might  come from Kazakhstan I mean we're in an  interconnected world geographically and  also in terms of whatever you know we  products we enjoy financial success that  these people at Davos have enjoyed it's  part of being connected internationally  connected it's these forces of  globalization yeah there have been sort  of disruptions certain types of jobs in  developed countries are no longer  available because they've been moved to  countries where you know wages are lower  but these are disruptions that we've  seen before people moved off the land  and used to be 95 percent of the people  on the land now it's 3 or 4 percent so  this is the kind of thing that it is not  unique to today but as I look at this  whole globalization thing and so these  people go to Davos to hobnob and rub  shoulders with other wealthy people and  to be seen at the bows and so forth and  it kind of reminds me of how the  polyglots get together and our Devoe's  which might be the polyglot conference  that richard cencon organizes or the  polyglot gathering that i attended that  bratislava or of course our own polyglot  gathering known as the long fest  festival day long  in montreal where people are happy to  get together and meet with people who  share a common interest and this  interest in languages to me is is as  rewarding in my  as this interest in money it affects  fewer people it's in a way perhaps more  selfish it's just those of us who are  learning languages of course we want to  encourage others to learn languages  whereas the business leaders at Davos  have a major impact on the economic  well-being of their employees or  customers and so forth so on and  interestingly you know Trump at Davos is  people want to have a selfie with Trump  and because they've seen him on TV so  often you know what  even me with my youtube presence you  know I was recently when I was in  Jerusalem I was in this market busy  market Friday at 2 o'clock there you  know mad shopping and they're about to  close for the Sabbath Shabbat and my  wife and I wanted to have a cup of  coffee when did this little coffee stand  and this guy says no so I said can we  have a macchiato  oh you're Steve Kaufman I watch you on  YouTube and of course if you've only  ever seen someone on YouTube or on  television when you see them live that's  kind of you know a bit of a of an event  so I guess the point I want to make is  languages now the other interesting  thing at Davos is you have different  countries there you have say Germany and  say India China okay two different  extremes in Germany the union's want to  have a twenty eight hour work week  already in Europe they work shorter  hours than in North America they have  longer holidays and now they want to  have this 20 hour  28 hour work week I don't know if  they'll get it I don't know what the  implications are but we used to have you  know ten-year-old children working 14  and 16 hours a day in the mines so it's  not impossible again as these changes  take place that ultimately we might have  a 28 hour work week you know it's like  it countries like Finland are  experimenting with a guaranteed income  to pay  so then the question is where do you  spend your free time once you've got so  much more free time what better place to  spend it than learning languages because  when you learn languages you can connect  you can connect geographically to  different parts of the world  you can connect historically there's no  question that as I study Arabic and I'm  only scratching a surface just getting  started I start to get a different  perspective on the Arab world and it is  my intention to continue from Arabic to  Farsi to Turkey Turkish on to Hindi and  since I already speak Chinese so then  I'm connected through the old Silk Road  into you know modern-day geography and a  connection back to history and so while  some people want to accumulate lots of  money and that has its benefits I think  there's a tremendous amount of reward in  learning languages learning languages  because of the things that we can learn  because of the sense of achievement that  it gives us the opportunity to connect  with people and with histories and with  cultures and you know we have the  example of Mark Zuckerberg who is the  founder of Facebook and was worth many  billions of dollars but what he really  wants to be able to do is to speak  Chinese so you know it's not evident  that those people at Davos with all  their money and their power and their  influence in terms of what they're able  to do every day with their lives how  they are able to enjoy every minute of  every day whether they are happier than  someone who is successful at learning  languages now maybe because I happen to  like learning languages and I have had a  certain amount of success doing that  that's not to say that I'm better than  someone who likes gardening or you know  whatever bird-watching I mean whatever  we choose to do if it gives us  satisfaction if it doesn't harm other  people it's a good thing but I would  like to  you know put in a plug for language  learning as a something that is  available to anyone because I definitely  believe that just as we can learn our  first language we can learn our second  third and fourth language if we put in  the time and if we are positive in our  attitude and so the rewards are great  it's a way to connect with the world  without having to go to the hosts and  without having to have a big bank  account so there you have it Davos  globalization and language learning bye  for now  oh and I forgot to mention that if you  would like to have a 30 minute  discussion with me about language  learning then please comment here about  what you would like to talk to me about  how you would like me to help you with  your language learning we are keeping  this little it's not a competition but  this event open and there will be a draw  on the 2nd of February bye for now  [Music]
 

